**Problem.** Multiple producer threads call `put(item)` and multiple consumers call `take()` on a bounded buffer of capacity N. `put` must block when full, `take` must block when empty, and the buffer must never be corrupted.

**Hand-rolled solution (one lock, two conditions)** in Java-like pseudocode:

```
class BoundedBuffer<T> {
  Queue<T> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
  int cap; Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();
  Condition notFull = lock.newCondition();
  Condition notEmpty = lock.newCondition();

  void put(T x) throws InterruptedException {
    lock.lock();
    try {
      while (q.size() == cap) notFull.await();
      q.add(x);
      notEmpty.signal();
    } finally { lock.unlock(); }
  }
  T take() throws InterruptedException {
    lock.lock();
    try {
      while (q.isEmpty()) notEmpty.await();
      T x = q.remove();
      notFull.signal();
      return x;
    } finally { lock.unlock(); }
  }
}
```

**Key points to explain:**
- Always wait in a **`while` loop**, not an `if`, to handle spurious wakeups and the case where another thread consumed the state first.
- `await` atomically releases the lock and re-acquires it on wake-up, so there is no lost wake-up between checking the condition and sleeping.
- Use **two conditions** so producers wake only consumers and the reverse; with a single condition you need `signalAll`, which wakes everyone unnecessarily.
- Release the lock in `finally`.

**Alternatives.** `ArrayBlockingQueue` or `LinkedBlockingQueue` in Java do exactly this; in Python `queue.Queue(maxsize)`. Two semaphores (empty slots and filled slots) plus a mutex also work. For high throughput consider a lock-free ring buffer (single producer, single consumer) or separate locks for head and tail (as `LinkedBlockingQueue` does).

**Extras:** support timeouts (`await(timeout)`), a clean shutdown (poison pill or a closed flag), and fairness. Test with many threads and verify counts and no deadlock.
